  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely from late \non Thanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is low, but travelers should continue \nto monitor this period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe general weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the \nweekend is favorable for active weather. This will likely impact\nholiday travel. \n\nUpper low currently diving over the Canadian Rockies will \ndeepen as it digs into the upper Midwest. As a shortwave gets \nejected into the southern plains. As the 12z GFS continues to \nkeep both features separate from each other as it continues to \nmove across the southern plains. The 06z Euro has the two \nfeatures merging as it moves up the Ohio River valley up to the \nNortheast. As the CMC in its latest run continues to keep the \ntwo features separate as it moves into the Northeast. \nUncertainty continues to be focused on timing of precipitation \nmoving in and how quickly a secondary low pressure can form near\nthe waters. \n\nAs far as ptypes, this also depends on how quickly precipitation\nmoves in. 850mb temps temps will still be above freezing at \nonset of precipitation though surface temps will be hovering \nright around the freezing mark. Cannot rule out cold air damming\nsetting up over favored areas from the Houlton area back toward\nthe Central Highlands late Thursday night and Friday morning. \nCannot rule out a brief period of freezing rain at onset, if \nonset can occur before sunrise. However, if precipitation comes \nlater in the day it will lower the chances of freezing rain that\ncould occur. That being said, road temps may still be below \nfreezing especially bridges. \n\nAdditional chances for wintry mix/ snow showers are possible through \nthe weekend, though confidence is low at this time. In summary, it \nis still early, but be aware of the potential for active weather \nthat could impact travel Thanksgiving evening through the weekend.\n\nMinor coastal flooding is possible mainly around the midday\nFriday high tide. This could come as overwash/wave runup on more\nexposed south/east facing shorelines. One mitigating factor is\nthat it tentatively appears that the greatest storm surge comes\nnear low tide Friday morning in between high tide cycles. We\nwill have to keep an eye on timing, though, as this could\ninfluence the degree of coastal flooding. Continue to check back\nfor updates.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder temperatures through Thursday with gusty\nnorthwest winds this afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nGusty northwest winds continue \ninto the early evening hours with wind gusts between 25 and 30 \nmph exacerbating the much colder temperatures this afternoon. \nHighs remain on track to reach to reach the low 40s in the \ncoastal Downeast with temperatures peaking in the low to mid 30s\nelsewhere, but apparent temperatures remain in the low to high \n20s across the area. The winds will continue to diminish, other \nthan the immediate coast, through the evening hours as skies \nclear under the high pressure moving into the region. This will \nallow temperatures to fall into the mid teens to low 20s across \nmuch of the area. Wednesday will again see colder temperatures \nwith similar highs and lows as today albeit with much lower \nwinds.",
